  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 147 PM MDT Thu Aug 27 2026\n\nShowers and thunderstorms have been ongoing for most of the day and \ncontinue early this afternoon. Much of southern and southeastern \nIdaho is included in the \"Marginal\" risk today from the SPC outlook. \nThe main threat will be wind gusts around 60 mph. It appears most of \nthis activity will be wrapped up around 9-10 PM tonight with the \nnext round of showers arriving early Friday morning. This Pacific \nmoisture is being funneled into our area from a combination of an \narea of high pressure over New Mexico and western Texas and an area \nof low pressure over northern Washington. Previous model runs wanted \nto bring the low a bit farther south into nearly central Idaho, \nthough now it is staying just a bit farther north. Not a significant \nchange, but one to keep watching. A track farther south could \ncontinue the trend of cooler temperatures and slightly increased \nprecipitation. Even with this slightly \"northern\" track, we can \nstill expect cooler temperatures overall and some measurable \nrainfall for much of the area. PWATs beginning Friday afternoon will \nbe pushing 0.80-1.10\" and a wetting rain is expected with these \nstorms on Friday and especially on Saturday. Isolated thunderstorms \nreturn for Friday afternoon with a little break before another round \nof widespread showers overnight Friday into Saturday morning. \nUncertainty lies within how strong afternoon convection will be \neither day based on how well the atmosphere is worked over in the \nmorning and how long it will have to \"recover.\" \n\nAs the area of low pressure moves eastward during the weekend, it \nwill take the precipitation with it. By Sunday afternoon, any \nleftover activity will be more confined to the Eastern Highlands. \nLooking at 48-hour precipitation amounts ending Sunday morning, the \nNBM shows about a 30-40 percent chance of at least a quarter inch of \nrainfall for areas like Burley, Carey, Arco, to Fort Hall. This may \nbe a bit overdone, but at least a tenth of an inch seems likely for \nmuch of the area. Precipitation amounts increase farther east you go \nwith a 60 to 80 percent chance of at least a quarter inch of \nrainfall for the bulk of the Central Mountains and the Eastern \nHighlands. There's about a 30-50 percent chance of at least a half \ninch of rain from Stanley to Hailey and about a 60 to 70 percent \nchance of the same from Preston to Victor to Island Park. \n\nHigh confidence remains in falling temperatures with today being the \nlast day we see highs in the upper 80s to low 90s for a bit. By \nFriday afternoon, highs will be nearly 10 degrees cooler before we \ndrop into the 70s for most on Saturday and low to mid 70s on Sunday. \nWe'll be back into the upper 70s and low 80s by Monday and Tuesday, \nbut it won't be quite as quick of a warm up as we've seen lately. \nLook for wind gusts to pick up on Saturday and Sunday, too, even \noutside of thunderstorm activity. Gusts will range from 25 to 35 mph \neach afternoon.",
